# The logic and its algebra

Formulas are built from variables and $`\bot` by $`\land`, $`\lor`,
$`\twoheadrightarrow` and $`\bigcirc`; $`\lnot A` is $`A \twoheadrightarrow \bot`
and $`\top` is $`\bot \twoheadrightarrow \bot`.  Derivability $`\Gamma \vdash A`
is natural deduction with the intuitionistic rules and, for the modality,

$$`\dfrac{\Gamma \vdash A}{\Gamma \vdash \bigcirc A}\;(\bigcirc I) \qquad\qquad \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash \bigcirc A \qquad A, \Gamma \vdash \bigcirc B}{\Gamma \vdash \bigcirc B}\;(\bigcirc E)`

{pcite}`FairtloughMendler1997`.  $`\bigcirc` is a nucleus in the sense of
{cite}`Macnab1981` (unit, multiplication, meets), the reading of
{cite}`Goldblatt1981`.  Constraint models have an intuitionistic preorder
$`R_i`, a modal preorder $`R_m \subseteq R_i` and fallible worlds, with
$`w \Vdash \bigcirc A` when every $`R_i`-successor of $`w` has an
$`R_m`-successor forcing $`A`; PLL is sound and complete for them.

$`\mathrm{Fm}_0` is the set of variable-free formulas and $`F_0` its quotient by
$`\dashv\vdash`, ordered by $`\vdash`: a Heyting algebra with the nucleus
$`\bigcirc`, generated by its bottom, and infinite.  By the usual Lindenbaum
argument (not mechanised) it is the initial Heyting algebra with a nucleus.

# Rungs and gaps

The rungs of the free Heyting algebra on one generator, described by
{cite}`Rieger1949` and {cite}`Nishimura1960`, with $`\bigcirc\bot` for the
generator, are

$$`t_0 = \bot,\quad t_1 = \bigcirc\bot,\quad t_2 = \lnot\bigcirc\bot,\quad t_{2k+3} = t_{2k+1} \lor t_{2k+2},\quad t_{2k+4} = t_{2k+3} \twoheadrightarrow t_{2k+1},`

and the gaps are $`g_k = \bigcirc t_{2k+1} \twoheadrightarrow t_{2k+1}`.  The
order among the rungs is decidable arithmetic: inclusion of their truth sets on
the Rieger–Nishimura ladder, checked by `rungLe`.  PROVED.

