# Pairs, intervals and threads

This vocabulary is defined on paper and used from here on.  A _pair_ is
$`(p, q)` with $`p < q` in $`F_0`; its _interval_ is
$`[p, q] = \{x : p \vdash x \vdash q\}` and its _open interval_
$`(p, q) = \{x : p < x < q\}`.  A _thread_ in $`(p, q)` is the image of an order
embedding of $`\mathbb{Q}` into $`(p, q)`.  In the _dyadic version_ the dyadic
rationals in $`[0, 1]` are embedded into $`[p, q]` with $`0 \mapsto p` and
$`1 \mapsto q`; the image of those in $`(0, 1)` is a thread, through the
order-isomorphism of Section 5 from $`\mathbb{Q}` onto them.  For sets of
elements, and threads in particular,

$$`T \parallel T' \iff \forall x \in T,\ \forall y \in T'.\ x \nvdash y \;\text{and}\; y \nvdash x,`

and a family of threads is _pairwise_ $`\parallel` when every two distinct
members are $`\parallel`.  `incomparable_copies` (Section 7) gives this for the
images of the dyadics strictly between $`0` and $`1`: the dyadic versions share
their ends $`p` and $`q`, which threads, as images of $`\mathbb{Q}` in the open
interval, never contain.

An interval $`[p, q]` with $`p < q` is at _level_ 1 if it is finite (for
example $`[\bot, t_5]`, with six elements); 2 if it contains a thread; 3 if it
contains a free distributive lattice on countably many generators, with $`p`
and $`q` as bottom and top; 4 if it contains a faithful copy of $`F_0` with $`p`
and $`q` as bottom and top, that is, with the operations relativised to
$`[p, q]` (implication $`(x \twoheadrightarrow y) \land q`, modality
$`\bigcirc x \land q`, bottom $`p`), the map

$$`h_{p,q}(\varphi) = \varphi[\bot := p] \land q`

is one-to-one on $`F_0`.  Pen arguments, not declarations of the library:
$`h_{p,q}` is a homomorphism for the relativised operations (by `subBot_deriv`
and the reduction lemmas of Section 3); 4 implies 3, as $`h_{p,q}` carries the
free distributive lattice of the odd gaps (`indep`, `lpoly_deriv_iff`) one-to-one
into $`[p, q]`; 3 implies 2 by the dyadic construction of Section 5, and level 3
gives countably many pairwise $`\parallel` threads by that of Section 7, both
mechanised for the odd gaps only; level 1 excludes level 2.  For every $`n` the
interval $`[\sigma^{n}(\bot), \top]` is at level 4 (Section 10):
$`h_{\sigma^{n}(\bot), \top}(\varphi) \dashv\vdash \sigma^{n}(\varphi)`, by the
pen identity $`\sigma^{n}(\varphi) = \varphi[\bot := \sigma^{n}(\bot)]`, and
$`\sigma^{n}` is one-to-one by `sigN_faithful`.

_Remark (pen, not mechanised)._  In a distributive lattice every interval with
more than four elements has a zigzag-connected open interval: two points
$`x, y \in (p, q)` are joined in at most three steps.  If $`x \lor y < q` or
$`x \land y > p`, one step through it.  Otherwise $`x` and $`y` are complements
in $`[p, q]`, so $`[p, q] \cong [p, x] \times [p, y]`; one factor has an interior
point, say $`p < w < x`, and $`x \ge w \le w \lor y \ge y` with $`w \lor y < q`.

